    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.get_common_neighbors - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.get_common_neighbors.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.get_common_neighbors()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get nodes that are neighbors of both specified nodes.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.get_common_neighbors()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get nodes that are neighbors of both specified nodes.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.get_common_neighbors()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Get nodes that are neighbors of both specified nodes.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nGet nodes that are neighbors of both specified nodes.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L29362-L29465){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.get_common_neighbors(\n    node_1,\n    node_2,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Common neighbors](../assets/graphs/get-common-neighbors.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Nodes B and E are outlined in the accent color; their two shared neighbors C and D are filled in accent, while A (only next to B) and F and G (only next to E) stay plain. The card lists N(B) = A, C, D and N(E) = C, D, F, G, and returns C and D.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nReturns the intersection of the two nodes' neighbor sets. Neighbors are taken with\n`mode=\"all\"`, so in a directed graph a node counts as a neighbor of `node_1=` (or\n`node_2=`) if there is an edge between them in either direction.\n\nThe number of common neighbors is one of the simplest and most effective structural\nsimilarity signals. Typical uses:\n\n- link prediction: two unconnected nodes with many shared neighbors are likely to become\n  connected (the basis of Jaccard and Adamic-Adar scores)\n- triangle detection: if `node_1=` and `node_2=` are adjacent, each common neighbor\n  closes a triangle with them\n- finding shared contacts, co-authors, or mutual friends\n\nThe two input nodes themselves are not returned unless one is a neighbor of both (for\nexample via a self-loop). The method does not modify the graph or the selection.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[node_1]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The first node, given as an integer node ID or a string label. An unknown ID or label\n    raises `ValueError`.\n\n<code>[node_2]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The second node, accepted in the same forms as `node_1=`. The operation is symmetric,\n    so swapping `node_1=` and `node_2=` gives the same set of nodes.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Node IDs that are neighbors of both `node_1=` and `node_2=`. Built from a set\n    intersection, so it contains no duplicates and its order is not guaranteed; sort it\n    if you need a stable order.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~get_neighbors`) \n\n:   The neighbors of a single node.\n\n[](`~jaccard_coefficient`) \n\n:   Shared neighbors as a fraction of both nodes' neighbors.\n\n[](`~adamic_adar_index`) \n\n:   Shared neighbors weighted by how few friends each has.\n\n[](`~dispersion`) \n\n:   How unconnected two nodes' shared neighbors are.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\n\"A\" and \"B\" are both joined to \"C\" and \"D\", so those are their common neighbors. \"E\" is\njoined only to \"A\":\n\n::: {#4772178c .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"C\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"A\", \"D\"), (\"B\", \"D\"), (\"A\", \"E\")]\n)\nshared = graph.get_common_neighbors(\"A\", \"B\")\n\nsorted(graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in shared)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n['C', 'D']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nIn a directed graph, direction is ignored. \"M\" is a successor of \"X\" and a predecessor of\n\"Y\", and it's still a common neighbor:\n\n::: {#dc142489 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nchain = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"X\", \"M\"), (\"M\", \"Y\")], directed=True)\n\n[chain.get_label_from_id(n) for n in chain.get_common_neighbors(\"X\", \"Y\")]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n['M']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A couple between two clubs\n\nIn a network of friendships among the members of a chess club and a climbing club, Hugo\nplays chess and his partner Tara climbs. They share four friends, two from each club:\n\n::: {#362e8fef .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfriends = ec.friends()\nshared = friends.get_common_neighbors(\"Hugo\", \"Tara\")\nclub = friends.get_node_attrs(\"club\")\n\n{friends.get_label_from_id(n): club[n] for n in shared}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n{'Ben': 'Chess', 'Jules': 'Chess', 'Wes': 'Climbing', 'Oona': 'Climbing'}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nNone of the four are friends with each other. Each of them knows the couple through a\ndifferent part of their life, which is what makes the tie between Hugo and Tara stand out:\n\n::: {#ec6ab4b3 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nany(friends.has_edge(u, v) for u in shared for v in shared if u != v)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\nFalse\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#3d78da31 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfriends.layout_kamada_kawai()\nfriends.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", label_by=\"label\",\n    highlight={\"accent\": [\"Hugo\", \"Tara\"], \"soft\": shared},\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-9f8a22df:#efc5b7;--ect-6c2f7015:#a78980;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-9f8a22df:#4c2e23;--ect-6c2f7015:#352018;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-2